<p>My ~/org/notes folder had nearly 4,000 files in it this morning. It now has 170. I deleted the rest.</p>
<!-- more -->
<p>Having lost my religion around &quot;zettelkasten&quot; and &quot;second brains&quot;, my notes started to look a little silly. Hundreds of nearly empty files with carefully-curated front matter are not helpful, no matter how many links between them have been created.</p>
<p>I mean, what use is a note for, say, &quot;Docker&quot;, which contains a link to the Docker website and Github page with a one-sentence summary like &quot;an open-source platform that uses operating system-level virtualization to package, deploy, and run applications inside lightweight, isolated environments called container&quot;. All that would take like 5 seconds to look up any time I need it. And backlinks? So what if I mentioned &quot;Docker&quot; in a half-dozen other files. It's all useless to me. I also don't need &quot;clippings&quot; from web pages or books if I haven't added any of my own thoughts about them. I'm only just now learning this.</p>
<p>It's not that I've been doing it wrong, but I'm not an academic. I'm not doing research for a book. I'm not studying for an exam. Those tasks might benefit from the whole link-everything-and-generate-a-cool-graph thing, but mine don't.</p>
<p>What I actually need from a note-taking system:</p>
<ul>
<li>tips and things I've learned (e.g. &quot;My Fedora post-install tasks&quot;)</li>
<li>information about people I know</li>
<li>projects I'm working on (e.g. &quot;Clean and improve rear landscaping (2026)&quot;)</li>
<li>a log of what I've done (e.g. &quot;Bought LED bulbs from Amazon (2700K)&quot; or &quot;Had dinner with Shelley and Jeff&quot;)</li>
</ul>
<p>Anything that wasn't any of those things has been deleted. It was a lot, and I feel good about it.</p>
